## Local setup

Before generation, complete the platform's developer onboarding checklist.
Then run the generated preflight and setup:

```bash
python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py
make check
```

Run `make help` for individual development, evaluation, and bundle targets.

Use `python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py --check-only` to verify tools,
authentication, SDK volume access, and compute-policy visibility without
installing anything. The setup never grants permissions or creates cloud
resources.

Replace every `replace-with-*` value in `aai-platform.yml` before running a
provider integration.

## How the agent works

- `src/app/tools.py` registers async code tools (plain Python) in the
  application-owned `AsyncToolRegistry`; for governed structured-data lookups,
  create Unity
  Catalog functions.
{{ if and (eq .include_serving "yes") (eq .model_provider "databricks") }}
  Ask the platform owner to grant the App service principal least-privilege
  `EXECUTE` on each function.
{{ else }}
  Declare each function with least-privilege `EXECUTE` access in the reviewed
  deployment resources you add later.
{{ end }}
- `src/app/agent.py` drives a bounded async loop over the provider-native
  client, executes async tools, and validates the final answer with the
  Pydantic contract in `src/app/schemas.py`. It records only meaningful,
  observable decisions: a provider-requested tool selection, convergence after
  tool evidence, or answer readiness without a tool. It never asks for or
  reconstructs hidden chain-of-thought.
- `src/app/controls.py` centralizes conservative defaults for input size, tool
  turns/fan-out/timeouts/output, model output tokens, streamed output
  characters, and the whole-request deadline. The local stream ceiling still
  applies if a provider ignores `max_tokens`. New teams inherit these bounds
  without learning provider-specific knobs; change them only with an SLO/cost
  decision.
- The prompt alias is resolved once at worker startup. Each request then loads
  that immutable version inside its active trace so MLflow records prompt
  lineage; MLflow caches the small Registry metadata lookup for five minutes.
- `src/app/feedback.py` keeps behavior evidence native: feedback,
  interventions, delayed business outcomes, and explicitly reviewed
  expectations are MLflow assessments on the originating trace. Intervention
  and outcome evidence is marked `review_required` and never mutates a
  dataset or training pipeline automatically.
- `aai-core` owns only the serializable request/response boundary; the native
  client, stream, tools, and loop remain recognizable application objects.
- Tests script provider-native responses and streams with async fakes, so they
  remain credential-free and exercise cancellation and cleanup.

## Behavior assurance model

Use four separate evidence layers when reviewing an agent:

| Layer | Question | Evidence in this project |
|---|---|---|
| Outcome | What happened at the end? | Structured output, correctness, safety, and schema validation |
| Behavior | How did the agent get there? | Decision, TOOL, LLM, intervention, and feedback evidence |
| Operations | How well did it operate? | Span status, errors, latency, token usage, and cost |
| Optional internal diagnostics | What supported provider signal is available? | Provider-native metadata when explicitly exposed and approved |

Outcome, behavior, and operations are the production assurance evidence.
Provider diagnostics are optional, must not be reconstructed, and cannot be a
runtime dependency.

The decision record is a concise application claim, not proof that an action
succeeded:

```text
decision.tool_selection [AGENT]
  selected_action = lookup_order_status
  reason = provider response explicitly requested this tool

lookup_order_status [TOOL]
  inputs/status/output = authoritative observed execution

decision_action_consistency [Assessment]
  verdict = independent comparison of the claim and execution

trace_execution_success [Assessment]
  verdict = independent root/TOOL status check, not a quality judgment
```

`decision.*` spans are native MLflow `AGENT` spans. Their bounded attributes
hold a goal, selected action, concise observable reason, evidence references,
and optional confidence. They do not contain prompts, documents, or hidden
reasoning. The default loop has no retry, fallback, or human-approval branch,
so it does not fabricate those decisions. The optional LangGraph recipe adds
such behavior only when the application genuinely implements it.

Tool scoring canonicalizes both application SDK spans (`span.name` plus
object `span.inputs`) and managed OTel GenAI spans (`gen_ai.tool.name` plus
`gen_ai.tool.call.arguments`). Missing or non-object arguments are scorer
errors rather than implicit empty arguments, so runtime portability never
weakens exact trajectory evaluation.

Production scorer registration has a narrower input contract than a release
evaluation row. A registered scorer receives the sampled trace, but no reviewed
benchmark `expectations`. The semantic rule behind
`decision_action_consistency` can be reused because it needs only the trace,
but the registered version must be self-contained notebook-defined `@scorer`
code. Registration serializes notebook code; importing
`decision_action_consistency_scorer()` from `app.tool_scoring` does not make the
factory and its helper dependencies serializable. By contrast,
`decision_tool_appropriateness` requires reviewed `expected_tool_calls`, so it
cannot be registered unchanged. Keep that scorer in dataset-backed development
and release evaluation.

Turning production evidence into a regression case is a reviewed data operation,
not scorer registration. Certified MLflow 3.15.1 has no public `promote_trace`
API. After minimization and human review, use the MLflow UI Add/Export flow or
native `dataset.merge_records(reviewed_traces)`. Trace conversion carries root
inputs/outputs, expectation Assessments, and source trace/session lineage into
the dataset row; it does not copy the full span tree. Normalize reviewed
behavior expectations such as `expected_tool_calls` when the gate needs them.
This repository adds no stable `aai-core` wrapper for that native surface;
live-validate the managed UI and conversion behavior in the target workspace.

## Release lifecycle

1. Edit `prompts/system_prompt.json`; register a version:
   `python scripts/register_prompt.py`. Record the returned immutable version
   and set `variables.prompt_version.default` in `databricks.yml` to that exact
   value. Do not put a mutable alias in deployment configuration.
2. Grow `evals/data/release_cases.json` — every case declares
   exact `expected_tool_calls` names and arguments (or an empty list for a
   no-tool case), so missing, repeated, mis-parameterized, or gratuitous
   calls fail the gate. Decision appropriateness reuses these same reviewed
   expectations; there is no duplicate decision ground-truth format.
3. Publish the reviewed cases to their native Unity Catalog EvaluationDataset:
   `python scripts/sync_dataset.py`. The command safely gets or creates the
   dataset, associates it with this application's experiment, and deliberately
   does not pass unsupported UC dataset tags. If cases are removed, choose a
   new versioned `DATASET_NAME`; sync is non-destructive.
4. Configure an approved `judge-model` endpoint that is distinct from the
   `general-chat` target, then run the gate:
   `make evaluate PROMPT_VERSION=<version>` — explicitly routed
   Correctness/Safety judges plus an MLflow-compatible exact scorer over
   traced TOOL spans, claimed-action consistency, and
   tool-decision appropriateness. `trace_execution_success` separately checks
   root and TOOL span status; it cannot prove outcome or decision quality. The
   required version must be the same value recorded in `databricks.yml`. The
   gate covers empty and repeated-call trajectories as well as names/arguments,
   with thresholds in
   `evals/gate_config.json` and baseline regression. Evaluation verdicts remain
   MLflow Assessments and are never written back into the original decision
   span. The gate loads and passes the native UC dataset, verifies it exactly
   matches the repository-reviewed suite, and records target/judge identities
   plus gate-policy and baseline digests. It also records an adopt or reject
   decision run and prints both the evaluation run ID and `decision_run_id`.
   The SDK's strict judge resolver runs before tracing, prompt loading, or agent
   construction, so a missing, placeholder, non-Databricks, or malformed
   endpoint fails locally. After a released run, append
   `EVALUATE_ARGS=--update-baseline`.
5. Materialize immutable release evidence from that passing run:
   `python scripts/create_release.py --prompt-version <n> --evaluation-run
   <evaluation-run-id> --world-version <source-snapshot>`. The v2 manifest records the
   World/Learning/Control digests, tool and execution limits, dataset digest,
   AI Platform manifest hash, readiness profile, budget policy, service
   levels, owner, and cost center. A default/manual **remote** bundle gate is
   deliberately diagnostic: its safe defaults record `source_commit=local-dev`
   and `source_state=unknown`, so it can show behavior but cannot mint a
   release. Use the included CI workflow, which first verifies a clean checkout
   and then injects the full Git SHA plus `GIT_DIRTY=false`. An advanced manual
   deployment must perform the same clean-tree check and explicitly set
   `BUNDLE_VAR_source_commit` to the full 40- or 64-character object ID and
   `BUNDLE_VAR_source_state=false`. `create_release.py` fails closed for a ref
   name, short SHA, dirty/unknown source, or a gate run from different code.
6. Promote the prompt (`python scripts/promote_prompt.py --version <n>
   --decision-run-id <decision-run-id> --to validation|production`). The script reloads
   and verifies the persisted decision artifact, tags, gate metrics, and exact
   prompt binding before moving either alias.
{{ if and (eq .include_serving "yes") (eq .model_provider "databricks") }}
7. Before enabling deployment, have the approved platform process create the
   Databricks App named `{{.application_name}}-dev`. App creation provisions
   its dedicated service principal. The platform owner must grant that
   identity only:

   - `CAN_EDIT` on experiment `{{.experiment_id}}`;
   - `CAN_QUERY` on serving endpoint `{{.model_deployment}}`;
   - `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, and `EXECUTE` on Prompt Registry function
     `{{.catalog}}.{{.schema}}.{{.prompt_name}}`; and
   - `READ VOLUME` on the SDK artifact volume represented by
     `{{.aai_core_volume}}`.

   The deploy identity also needs the externally approved rights required to
   update this App. Bundle state is identity-scoped, so perform the one-time binding
   inside the CI identity's manual workflow run:

   ```bash
   gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main -f target=dev \
     -f bind_app={{.application_name}}-dev
   ```

   `resources/agent_app.yml` deliberately contains no permission-bearing App
   resource bindings. The deploy workflow verifies that the App already
   exists before `bundle deploy`, so CI cannot become the identity or
   permissions bootstrap.

8. Deploy the bundle, run the workspace gate, and only then start or restart
   the externally provisioned Databricks App:

   ```bash
   databricks bundle deploy -t dev
   databricks bundle run release_gate -t dev
   databricks bundle run agent_app -t dev
   ```

   `src/app/endpoint.py`, `start_server.py`, and `app.yaml` expose the native
   MLflow Responses API contract. The bundle passes the same
   `${var.prompt_version}` to the `release_gate` job and to the App as
   `AAI_PROMPT_VERSION`; App startup rejects a missing, invalid, or alias-like
   value and `ToolAgent` loads only that exact version.

   The three commands above use the safe diagnostic provenance defaults when
   run manually. That is intentional for a new team: use the CI-produced gate
   run for release evidence unless your platform process explicitly attests
   and injects the clean full-SHA values described in step 5.
9. Before calling scorer `register()` / `start()`, complete the managed
   production-monitoring preflight: confirm Production Monitoring (Beta) is
   enabled; confirm the selected experiment is receiving the deployed agent's
   traces and has an approved serverless budget policy; and, when that
   experiment stores traces in Unity Catalog, confirm an available SQL
   warehouse plus the required catalog, schema, and trace-table permissions.
   `notebooks/02_enable_monitoring.py` fails closed until you explicitly confirm
   that checklist. It does not enable Beta, attach a budget policy, create a
   warehouse, or grant permissions. Then register the low-rate sampled scorer
   from the notebook and attach user feedback to traces. Registered scorers see
   a trace, not a benchmark row: define any trace-only consistency scorer as
   self-contained notebook `@scorer` code, and do not register the
   expectation-dependent appropriateness scorer unchanged. Use stable
   group/system assessment source IDs rather than personal emails, and curate
   only human-reviewed failures back into release cases.
{{ else }}
7. Serving is out of scope for this generated configuration. The bundle still
   deploys and runs `release_gate`; there is no `agent_app` resource. Route the
   model through a governed Databricks endpoint and regenerate (or add
   equivalent reviewed resources) before introducing an HTTP deployment.
{{ end }}

Pull-request CI runs only the deterministic checks
(`evals/offline_checks.py`) — it never holds credentials. The platform team
must onboard this repository (main-branch federated credential, service
principal, constrained compute, serving permissions for the deploy step,
non-secret repository variables) before the deploy workflow is enabled.

## Optional durable LangGraph orchestration

The default agent stays framework-neutral so the SDK does not hide native
MLflow or provider behavior. If the application needs durable checkpoints,
replay, or human approval, follow `recipes/langgraph/README.md`. The recipe
uses native LangGraph state APIs, injected persistent checkpoint/store
backends, an interrupt before side effects, and an application idempotency
key. Review decisions are strict payloads with reasons, and
`recipes/langgraph-lakebase/README.md` supplies the production
checkpointer/store wiring against Lakebase plus user-scoped memory tools
with decision lineage. `aai-core` continues to own resource context, trace
policy, evaluation, and release evidence rather than wrapping the graph.
